// A `near` whose statements write goes along its rows by blocks of 64:
// which rows are within reach one bit each, then the statements for each
// of those (math.ctpop, math.cttz); one whose statements only read and
// set vars goes row by row, as LLVM does many rows at once
// (Inputs/near_writes.ent). Each dot's list written so is the list the
// reading `near` sees, in the same order, within less than a cell, more,
// and more than half the box, where a run of rows has up to 200. The
// same on one core and on all. The function with the blocks prefers no
// gathers (ent.no_gather, see ent-emit-llvm).
// RUN: ent-translate --import-ent %S/Inputs/near_writes.ent -I %S/../../devices \
// RUN:   | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=IR
// RUN: ent-translate --import-ent %S/Inputs/near_writes.ent -I %S/../../devices \
// RUN:   | ent-opt --ent-lower-to-loops | FileCheck %s --check-prefix=LOW
// RUN: %ent run %S/Inputs/near_writes.ent 2>/dev/null > %t.one
// RUN: %ent run --parallel %S/Inputs/near_writes.ent 2>/dev/null > %t.all
// RUN: cmp %t.one %t.all
// RUN: FileCheck %s --input-file=%t.one

// IR-LABEL: ent.system @count(
// IR-NOT:   math.ctpop
// IR-LABEL: ent.system @fill(
// IR:       math.ctpop
// IR:       math.cttz
// IR:       ent.buffer.set

// LOW: func.func private @count({{.*}}) attributes {ent.trace = "system"}
// LOW: func.func private @fill({{.*}}) attributes {ent.no_gather,

// CHECK:      same lists 400 of 400, pairs 1599
// CHECK-NEXT: same lists 400 of 400, pairs 17645
// CHECK-NEXT: same lists 400 of 400, pairs 101042
